From fb73ad2bfeeaa52c75c899f31edea0f64e0c93cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Travis Gilbert <1travisgilbert@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 09:58:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] feat(gpui_web): mirror accessibility, position the IME
 caret, deliver file drops

Three web-platform gaps that HANDOFF-THEOREMWEB-SHELL-1.1 (M1, M2, M3) needs
closed before a GPUI canvas can be the whole product surface in a browser.

M1 -- accessibility. GPUI already builds a complete accesskit TreeUpdate every
frame; on wasm nothing consumed it, because `a11y_init` was gated out of the
wasm build. Removing that gate is the wiring; `gpui_web::a11y::WebA11yAdapter`
is the consumer. It maintains a hidden DOM subtree beside the canvas -- one
element per node, ARIA role mapped from `accesskit::Role`, plus label,
description, value, state and absolute bounds so assistive technology
hit-tests where the pixels are. `pointer-events: none` keeps pointer input on
the canvas. Focus ownership stays on the hidden input, with
`aria-activedescendant` tracking `TreeUpdate.focus`, matching GPUI's own
active-descendant model. DOM `click` and `focusin` dispatch back as
`accesskit::Action::Click` and `Focus` through one delegated listener each, so
node churn allocates no closures. One polite live region announces label
changes on nodes flagged live.

The adapter diffs against its own retained map rather than through
`accesskit_consumer`. `A11yNodeBuilder::finalize` always emits a whole tree --
`tree: Some(...)` and every node, every frame -- so a consumer whose purpose is
applying incremental updates to a retained tree would have nothing to apply.
Diffing where the DOM writes happen is what actually keeps a steady frame free.

M2 -- IME. `update_ime_position` was a no-op, so the browser opened candidate
windows at the hidden input's 1x1 box in the viewport corner. It now moves that
input to the caret. `text_input_state_changed` sets `inputmode`/`enterkeyhint`
so mobile browsers raise the software keyboard only for editable content.

M3 -- file drops. `PlatformInput::FileDrop` carries `ExternalPaths`, and a
browser has no path to give. Rather than synthesize one no consumer could open,
`drop` reads `DataTransfer.files`, awaits each `File.arrayBuffer`, and delivers
`WebFileDrop { name, mime, bytes, position }` on `web_file_drops()`, for a host
to turn into whatever its native FileDrop handler produces.

Upstream notes
- `send_blocking` is gated off wasm; on an unbounded channel it can never
  block, so these three sites use `try_send`, which says the same thing and
  compiles everywhere. Worth taking upstream on its own.
- The honest fix for M3 is a `PlatformInput::FileDrop` that can carry bytes
  instead of paths. That is a GPUI API change; this channel is the same
  delivery without it.
- `TextInputStateChange` carries no input *kind*, so `inputmode` can only be
  "text" or "none". A per-field hint needs the input handler to describe
  itself.
